  "worked_solution": "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{\u2032})=({y}^{\u2032}-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}-x{y}^{4}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x)y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{\u2032}+\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({y}^{\u2032}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{\u2032}+g({y}^{\u2032})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{\u2032})=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({y}^{\u2032}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
